Chronic Low Back Pain (Radicular Overlap)
Complex Fatigue Entities
Persistent neurogenic pain post-disc herniation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-006
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe radiating lower limb pain and acute functional immobility to rule out cord compression or progressive neurological deficit.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified sudden exacerbation of intractable leg pain and localized nerve root inflammation, rendering the patient unable to walk or sleep. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to exclude acute neurosurgical emergencies and provide potent pharmacological relief prior to the next in-hours period to prevent systemic decompensation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Lightning keeps shooting down my leg, and I can't even stand up to get to the bathroom.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a progressive 48-hour exacerbation of known chronic low back pain following a prior disc herniation. The current episode is characterized by unremitting, shooting leg pain along the L5 dermatome. Patient describes localized inflammation around the nerve root, causing severe hyperalgesia. The pain is electric and burning, continuously waking the patient and preventing functional ambulation.
Functional Status: Temporarily bedbound due to severe pain upon weight-bearing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; unable to access toilet or hydration safely tonight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, guarding lower back, shifting frequently in bed.
Physical Exam: Persistent neurogenic pain post-disc herniation evident. Straight leg raise positive at 30 degrees on the affected side. Hyperesthesia noted over the L5 dermatome. Motor strength globally 5/5 in lower limbs; reflexes symmetrical. No saddle anesthesia; perianal sensation intact.
Environmental Observations: Obstacles observed between bed and bathroom; immediate fall risk.
Medication Reconciliation: Maximum doses of over-the-counter NSAIDs and paracetamol consumed with no clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Low Back Pain (Radicular Overlap) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cauda equina syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact bowel/bladder control and normal perianal sensation.

Acute complete disc extrusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of dense motor deficit or new reflex loss.

Deep vein thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Unilateral radiating pain follows a nerve root pattern rather than diffuse calf swelling; negative Homan's sign.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s acute presentation of severe radicular pain and functional immobility posed an immediate risk for falls and necessitated urgent clinical differentiation from cauda equina syndrome. Given the failure of maximum OTC analgesia and the high level of distress, delaying medical intervention until regular clinic hours would have resulted in an unacceptable pain crisis and possible traumatic fall. Intramuscular analgesia and nerve-modulating therapy were urgently required in the home setting.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM for acute inflammatory suppression. Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg nocte to modulate neuropathic firing. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Doxycycline (MMP-inhibitor effect) and PEA.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the neuropathic pain medication exactly two hours before bed, you will actively suppress the nerve misfiring, directly rewarding yourself with restorative sleep and reduced morning leg pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for nerve gliding exercises once acute phase settles.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urinal provided for bedside use tonight to prevent falls.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for MRI lumbar spine consideration and pain management review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to Emergency Department via 000 if new leg weakness develops, saddle anesthesia occurs, or bowel/bladder control is lost.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; recurrent structural damage cannot be definitively excluded without neuroimaging.
Clinical Course: Radicular flares can last several days to weeks. Medications will blunt the severity but will not immediately eliminate the neuropathic pain.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if your leg completely gives out or if you wet yourself without realizing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain is caused by an inflamed, hypersensitive nerve root; the new medication stabilizes the electrical signals in that nerve.
Actionable Steps: Restrict activities to flat bed rest tonight. Tomorrow, attempt brief, 5-minute walks to prevent secondary muscle spasm, which positively reinforces overall spinal mobility.

